lima/ppir: let the result combiner multiply a vector by a scalar (a third multiply port)

Blob evidence. The Mali-400 PP's result combiner can take dest_vec = src_vec = 1, in which case the
opcode field is reused for a vector argument's source and swizzle (arg1), the scalar argument stays
arg0 and the destination is a masked vector. lima's own codegen.h already describes that encoding and
its disassembler prints it (`mul.s2 <dest> <scalar> <vector>`), but the scheduler never allowed a
non-scalar destination in the slot, so the vector form was dead code.

The blob uses the slot that way in nearly every program; captured instructions
(/var/tmp/mali-re/capture/demo/compare-26.2.6/, decoded field by field with beat-shader/ppdec.py):
  - program 40 `vec4(t.rgb * u_gain, t.a)`, one instruction:
      load.v ^discard 0.xy, texld 0, load.u 1.x, mov.s1 $0.w ^texture.w,
      mul.s2 $0.xyz ^uniform.x ^texture.xyzz, sync, stop
    combine field: dest_vec=1 src_vec=1 dest=$0 mask=7 arg0=uniform.x arg1=texture.xyzz
  - program 4 (the 5-tap blur) at @18, the x0.3162 scale of a whole vec4 riding along with the
    vec4 accumulator's add: `add.v1 $2 ^texture $0, mul.s2 $0 ^const0.x $1`
  - program 43 at @31 and the reference UI rounded rect (capture/games prog 3) at @42
    `mul.s2 $1 $0.y ^uniform`

What lima did. ppir_instr_insert_node() rejected the combine slot unless ppir_target_is_scalar(dest),
so every vector-times-scalar multiply went to the vec4 multiply slot and needed its own instruction
when that slot was taken. codegen also hardcoded src[0] as the scalar operand and src[1] as the
vector one, which is the wrong way round for NIR's usual fmul(vec, scalar) order.

This patch adds ppir_combine_mul_scalar_src(): the node fits the slot when it is a two-source
multiply with no shift, one source whose value is the same for every written component (arg0
addresses a single register component) and no negate/absolute on the other one (the vector form has
no modifier bits for arg1). The scheduler uses it as the gate and codegen uses it to order the
operands, so both agree.

Effect. The Demo's PP instructions fall from 48 to 46 (program 4: 7 -> 6 = the blob's, program 43:
8 -> 7 = the blob's), code 289 -> 278 words. Fill-weighted against the blob's per-job PP counters
(beat-shader/fillest.py): finale 10.76 -> 10.29 ms per frame, tunnel 12.57 -> 12.40, chrome
13.16 -> 13.01, horizon 13.61 -> 13.46. reference UI counts unchanged on their own (the saving there needs
0012 as well).

Pictures: BIT-IDENTICAL. The same multiply of the same two fp16 operands, moved to another unit;
multiplication is commutative and exact under round-to-nearest, so swapping the operand order
changes nothing. No arithmetic is reassociated and no rounding is added or removed.

Applies to Mesa 26.2.6 + 0001-0007.
diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/pp/codegen.c b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/pp/codegen.c
index dd04c4b..8a8207d 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/pp/codegen.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/pp/codegen.c
@@ -605,12 +605,20 @@ static void ppir_codegen_encode_combine(ppir_node *node, void *code)
       f->vector.dest = index >> 2;
       f->vector.mask = dest->write_mask << dest_shift;
 
-      ppir_src *src = alu->src;
+      /* arg0 is the scalar operand, arg1 the vector one; which source of the
+       * multiply is which is decided by ppir_combine_mul_scalar_src(), the same
+       * test the scheduler used to put the node here. */
+      int scl = ppir_combine_mul_scalar_src(node);
+      assert(scl >= 0);
+      if (scl < 0)
+         scl = 0;
+
+      ppir_src *src = alu->src + scl;
       f->scalar.arg0_src = get_scl_reg_index(src, 0);
       f->scalar.arg0_absolute = src->absolute;
       f->scalar.arg0_negate = src->negate;
 
-      src = alu->src + 1;
+      src = alu->src + 1 - scl;
       index = ppir_target_get_src_reg_index(src);
       f->vector.arg1_source = index >> 2;
       f->vector.arg1_swizzle = encode_swizzle(src->swizzle, index & 0x3, dest_shift);
diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/pp/instr.c b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/pp/instr.c
index 0befd2c..eeeb19f 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/pp/instr.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/ir/pp/instr.c
@@ -57,6 +57,45 @@ void ppir_instr_add_dep(ppir_instr *succ, ppir_instr *pred)
    list_addtail(&dep->succ_link, &pred->succ_list);
 }
 
+/* The result combiner can also work as a third multiply port: with dest_vec and
+ * src_vec both set, its opcode field is reused for a vector argument's source
+ * and swizzle (arg1), the scalar argument stays arg0 and the destination is a
+ * masked vector. That is the Mali blob's `mul.s2 $0.xyz <scalar> <vector>`,
+ * which it emits in nearly every program (e.g. the Demo's blur, program 4, and
+ * its tint, program 40).
+ *
+ * Returns the index of the source the combiner takes as its scalar operand, or
+ * -1 when the node does not fit the slot. arg0 addresses one register component
+ * (swizzle[0]), so that source's value must be the same for every written
+ * component; the vector argument has no negate/absolute bits in this form and
+ * the multiply has no shift field (the opcode bits are the vector argument).
+ */
+int ppir_combine_mul_scalar_src(ppir_node *node)
+{
+   if (node->op != ppir_op_mul)
+      return -1;
+
+   ppir_alu_node *alu = ppir_node_to_alu(node);
+   if (alu->num_src != 2 || alu->shift)
+      return -1;
+
+   for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
+      ppir_src *scl = alu->src + i, *vec = alu->src + 1 - i;
+
+      if (scl->swizzle[0] != scl->swizzle[1] ||
+          scl->swizzle[0] != scl->swizzle[2] ||
+          scl->swizzle[0] != scl->swizzle[3])
+         continue;
+
+      if (vec->negate || vec->absolute)
+         continue;
+
+      return i;
+   }
+
+   return -1;
+}
+
 void ppir_instr_insert_mul_node(ppir_node *add, ppir_node *mul)
 {
    ppir_instr *instr = add->instr;
@@ -343,22 +382,22 @@ bool ppir_instr_insert_node(ppir_instr *instr, ppir_node *node)
             if (instr->slots[PPIR_INSTR_SLOT_BRANCH])
                continue;
 
-            if (!ppir_target_is_scalar(dest))
-               continue;
             /* Combiner doesn't have pipeline destination */
             if (dest->type == ppir_target_pipeline)
                continue;
 
-            /* No modifiers on vector output */
-            if (node->op == ppir_op_mul && dest->modifier != ppir_outmod_none)
-               continue;
+            if (node->op == ppir_op_mul) {
+               /* No modifiers on vector output */
+               if (dest->modifier != ppir_outmod_none)
+                  continue;
 
-            /* No modifiers on vector source on combiner */
-            if (ppir_node_get_src_num(node) == 2) {
-               ppir_src *src = ppir_node_get_src(node, 1);
-               if (src->negate || src->absolute)
+               /* scalar x vector multiply: needs a scalar operand, and no
+                * modifiers on the vector one */
+               if (ppir_combine_mul_scalar_src(node) < 0)
                   continue;
             }
+            else if (!ppir_target_is_scalar(dest))
+               continue;
          }
 
          if (pos == PPIR_INSTR_SLOT_ALU_SCL_MUL ||
